                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### A Number After Is Greater\n\nWhen a number comes after another, it is greater. We write this relationship with the symbol (>), so **21 > 20** means “21 is greater than 20”.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "da732905-3ece-495a-807a-fff8b3dd6e3c",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "da732905-3ece-495a-807a-fff8b3dd6e3c",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "happy",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "0fe4b20d-1bd5-4f36-9d0b-87c10881ff8d",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=0fe4b20d-1bd5-4f36-9d0b-87c10881ff8d",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "4e3c3945-0bf4-4040-9735-546541b27a50",
               "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
               "page": "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Less.html",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### A Number Before Is Smaller\n\nThe opposite is also true: a number that comes before another is smaller. We use the symbol (<), so **20 < 21** means “20 is smaller than 21”.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "cc4f28b3-3c12-4682-9d44-2d90b34f86a3",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "cc4f28b3-3c12-4682-9d44-2d90b34f86a3",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "thinking",
         "source_menu": [],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Let Us Think!\n\nOn the ruler, the value 100 is placed after the value 20.\n\nDoes this mean that 100 is larger than 20?",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "0f696e6f-1b3c-401e-b5fb-7d08a8e82bb5",
         "prev": "",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": "yes",
         "score": 2,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "0f696e6f-1b3c-401e-b5fb-7d08a8e82bb5",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "shocking",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "0fe4b20d-1bd5-4f36-9d0b-87c10881ff8d",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=0fe4b20d-1bd5-4f36-9d0b-87c10881ff8d",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "c47a586f-6053-4b8e-8f9b-828cd6220110",
               "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
               "page": "https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Greater.html",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### The Same Logic\n\nEvery number that comes after another is bigger, and every number that comes before another is smaller — no matter how far apart they are.\n\nWhen the gap between two numbers is huge, mathematics offers a shortcut: (≫) reads “much greater than”, as in **1000000 ≫ 20**, and (≪) reads “much less than”, as in **20 ≪ 1000000**.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "9b524d17-d800-41d4-be1a-45541a9babd7",
